{"date": "2026-11-10", "post_id": 1509570, "title": "Saturn Behind the Moon", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-10-saturn-behind-the-moon/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Sometimes Saturn disappears. It doesn't really go away, of course -- it just\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-april-29-saturns-rings-appear-to-disappear/\">disappears</a>\u00a0from our view when\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-september-5-earth-and-moon/\">Earth's Moon</a>\u00a0moves in front. Such a\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/\">Saturn</a>ian eclipse, more formally called an\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-28-the-mutual-occultation-of-ganymede-and-io/\">occultation</a>, was visible in early 2025 along a small\u00a0<a href=\"https://in-the-sky.org/news/occultations/occultation_20250104_1652_visibility.png\">swath</a>\u00a0of\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/\">Earth</a>\u00a0-- from\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenland\">Greenland</a>\u00a0through central\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europe\">Europe</a>\u00a0and into northern\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Africa\">Africa</a>. The\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/194524290@N05/54963364007/\">featured image</a>\u00a0is a digital fusion of three images, the first of just\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/apod-2026-august-23-cassini-approaches-saturn/\">Saturn</a>\u00a0before it disappeared, and then two more of the Moon with\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2020-august-24-crescent-moon-hdr/\">different exposure</a>\u00a0lengths. The images were taken from\u00a0<a href=\"https://youtu.be/z6hf3TY5LZU\">Sardinia</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy\">Italy</a>, after sunset. The bright dot near Saturn is not a\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/apod-2026-august-11-six-moons-of-saturn/\">moon of Saturn</a>, but rather the star\u00a0<a href=\"https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/85_Aquarii\">85 Aquarii</a>, which is further behind Saturn than Saturn is behind the Moon. The next time the\u00a0<a href=\"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/5587/\">Moon</a>\u00a0moves in front of Saturn, as visible from\u00a0<a href=\"https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcRI9kyfDjt8-b4RVVADhbiFfBl5MR8Rl16peGoUYAuP0PwITdzj7rYB1EnN\">some place on Earth</a>, will be in 2031.\u00a0<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:\u00a0</strong>small but mighty", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/194524290@N05/\">Roberto Ortu</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/194524290@N05/\">Roberto Ortu</a>", "alt": "The curving surface of the Moon occupies the lower half of the image. Just above center the planet Saturn appears, far in the distance.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-10-saturn-behind-the-moon/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Moonsaturn6_Ortu_2124.jpg?w=2124&h=1582&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 10 - Saturn Behind the Moon</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-10-saturn-behind-the-moon/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Sometimes Saturn disappears. It doesn&#039;t really go away, of course -- it just\u00a0disappears\u00a0from our view when\u00a0Earth&#039;s Moon\u00a0moves in front. Such a\u00a0Saturnian eclipse, more formally called an\u00a0occultation, was visible in early 2025 along a small\u00a0swath\u00a0of\u00a0Earth\u00a0-- from\u00a0Greenland\u00a0through central\u00a0Europe\u00a0and into northern\u00a0Africa. The\u00a0featured image\u00a0is a digital fusion of three images, the first of just\u00a0Saturn\u00a0before it disappeared, and then two more of the Moon with\u00a0different exposure\u00a0lengths. The images were taken from\u00a0Sardinia,\u00a0Italy, after sunset. The bright dot near Saturn is not a\u00a0moon of Saturn, but rather the star\u00a085 Aquarii, which is further behind Saturn than Saturn is behind the Moon. The next time the\u00a0Moon\u00a0moves in front of Saturn, as visible from\u00a0some place on Earth, will be in 2031.\u00a0Tomorrow&#039;s picture:\u00a0small but mighty\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"saturn behind the moon\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Saturn Behind the Moon\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-10-saturn-behind-the-moon/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Moonsaturn6_Ortu_2124.jpg?w=2124&#038;h=1582&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Moonsaturn6_Ortu_2124.jpg?w=2124&#038;h=1582&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"The curving surface of the Moon occupies the lower half of the image. Just above center the planet Saturn appears, far in the distance.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-10T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-09T13:44:38-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Saturn Behind the Moon\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Moonsaturn6_Ortu_2124.jpg?w=2124&#038;h=1582&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 10<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Moonsaturn6_Ortu_2124.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Moonsaturn6_Ortu_2124.jpg\" alt=\"The curving surface of the Moon occupies the lower half of the image. Just above center the planet Saturn appears, far in the distance.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Saturn Behind the Moon </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/194524290@N05/\">Roberto Ortu</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Sometimes Saturn disappears. It doesn't really go away, of course -- it just\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-april-29-saturns-rings-appear-to-disappear/\">disappears</a>\u00a0from our view when\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-september-5-earth-and-moon/\">Earth's Moon</a>\u00a0moves in front. Such a\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/\">Saturn</a>ian eclipse, more formally called an\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-28-the-mutual-occultation-of-ganymede-and-io/\">occultation</a>, was visible in early 2025 along a small\u00a0<a href=\"https://in-the-sky.org/news/occultations/occultation_20250104_1652_visibility.png\">swath</a>\u00a0of\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/\">Earth</a>\u00a0-- from\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenland\">Greenland</a>\u00a0through central\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europe\">Europe</a>\u00a0and into northern\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Africa\">Africa</a>. The\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/194524290@N05/54963364007/\">featured image</a>\u00a0is a digital fusion of three images, the first of just\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/apod-2026-august-23-cassini-approaches-saturn/\">Saturn</a>\u00a0before it disappeared, and then two more of the Moon with\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2020-august-24-crescent-moon-hdr/\">different exposure</a>\u00a0lengths. The images were taken from\u00a0<a href=\"https://youtu.be/z6hf3TY5LZU\">Sardinia</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy\">Italy</a>, after sunset. The bright dot near Saturn is not a\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/apod-2026-august-11-six-moons-of-saturn/\">moon of Saturn</a>, but rather the star\u00a0<a href=\"https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/85_Aquarii\">85 Aquarii</a>, which is further behind Saturn than Saturn is behind the Moon. The next time the\u00a0<a href=\"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/5587/\">Moon</a>\u00a0moves in front of Saturn, as visible from\u00a0<a href=\"https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcRI9kyfDjt8-b4RVVADhbiFfBl5MR8Rl16peGoUYAuP0PwITdzj7rYB1EnN\">some place on Earth</a>, will be in 2031.\u00a0<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:\u00a0</strong>small but mighty\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-9-rigel-and-the-witch-head-nebula/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261110/html"}